<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "firesquire">
<!ENTITY pkg       "firesquire">
<!ENTITY author    "Junker der Provinz">
<!ENTITY version   "1.5.2">
<!ENTITY launch    "Main">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/main">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/plugin/firesquire.plg">
<!ENTITY pkgURL    "https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/firesquire/releases/download/v&version;/&pkg;-&version;.txz">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&pkg;">
<!ENTITY plgPath   "/boot/config/plugins/&pkg;">
<!ENTITY sha256 "d14cf844079d0da1fbe88de96b37f33b188387a7b4fcf2ab149e09502c4db690">
<!ENTITY helperVer "v1.3.1">
<!ENTITY helperURL "https://github.com/junkerderprovinz/parleyport/releases/download/&helperVer;">
<!ENTITY fortressSHA "3feef84709a1e62c68d6c72cac50cec81f624c4944b7e471044b542c154f7b09">
<!ENTITY notifySHA "54e6916df1c2e6ec0fc39dde97acb50cece2d7e1ad999d6b6445ba5bebca5dba">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;"
        author="&author;"
        version="&version;"
        launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        min="6.10.0"
        support="https://forums.unraid.net/topic/199508-support-junkerderprovinz-firesquire/">

<!-- Plugins-tab logo via the ICON element + local path (name-independent, the
     FanCtrlPlus convention). -->
<ICON>
/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&pkg;/images/&pkg;.png
</ICON>

  <CHANGES>
## 1.5.2
🎨 **Design**
- **Channels from GlimStone's catalog.** In the window that adds a channel, mail shows SMTP as its second line, the search also knows other words for a service, such as HTTP for the webhook, and the webhook comes last. Back from a channel's form marks the tile you picked before and puts the focus on it.
- **A window that fits the form.** Once you pick a tile, the window narrows to the form's width, and a click beside it keeps what you typed. The test sits in the window's foot and shows its result next to the button.
- **A search with its own clear button.** The search field carries a magnifier, and a small grey button empties it.
- **GlimStone 4.7.0.** With TowerTorch, quiet buttons in the channel window hover in its accent colour.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Tile names keep their descenders.** The bottom of a g, p or y in a tile's name in the channel window is no longer cut off.
- **Fortress helpers 1.3.1.** Two apps that first meet while one of them restarts still find each other.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The clear button of the search in all 26 languages.**

## 1.5.1
⚡ **Improved**
- **Fortress helpers 1.3.0.** The notify helper itself sends the all-clear to channels that only take failures.
- **The all-clear goes straight to those channels.** FireSquire marks it as a recovery, so it no longer needs a short-lived copy of your channels set to take everything.

## 1.5.0
✨ **Added**
- **A Health tab for the whole server.** Eight areas each get a light of their own: Disks, Array and pools, Docker, VMs, Flash, Network, Fortress and System. FireSquire keeps 30 days of history per area and tells you when one gets worse.
- **SMART trends per disk.** FireSquire remembers each disk's SMART values and warns when a sector count goes up. Sleeping disks stay asleep, so a scheduled check does not spin up the array.
- **A check before the array stops.** Stopping the array from Main or the Dashboard now waits for the same check as a reboot. It names what still holds the array: programs with open files, mounts and images inside it, VMs without a connected guest agent, running containers and a short time-out.
- **The flash drive and the certificate.** FireSquire watches the flash drive's read errors and free space, and it tells you before Unraid's certificate expires.
- **A time-out that fits your server.** After an unclean shutdown, FireSquire reads how long your VMs and containers took to stop last time and suggests the shutdown time-out in seconds.
- **Advice on share and plugin settings,** in a group of its own. Health and configuration findings stay out of the reboot verdict.
- **TollRoll can send your messages.** When your fortress has a TollRoll that collects notifications, FireSquire hands every message to it, and the Notifications card says so. Without TollRoll, or when it does not take a message, FireSquire sends through its own channels. Healthchecks pings always come from FireSquire.
- **Every message links to FireSquire's settings,** and the test message arrives in Unraid's language.
- **The all-clear reaches every channel.** When the trouble is over, channels that only take failures hear about it too.
- **FireSquire's status for TollRoll,** with its logo, written after every check and refreshed every hour.
- **Discord on the Help card.** The card offers GitHub and the FireSquire Discord server in place of the forum.

🎨 **Design**
- **Channels picked from tiles.** Add a channel in a window with a search and the services' logos, and fill in its fields right there. It is the same card as in ShipLog and TollRoll, and every change is saved at once.
- **A real time picker for the schedule.** Pick the hour and the minute from two lists, and the check runs at exactly that time.
- **Cleaner rows.** Fields, switches and buttons stand at the end of their row, every button shows its icon, an open list shows plain rows with a check mark, and no field has a frame.
- **The same info bubble as everywhere else.** The (i) and its bubble look like they do in your other apps.
- **GlimStone 4.5.5.** Buttons, tabs and choices light in a soft tint of your accent when the pointer reaches them. With TowerTorch they take its hover colour and its accent ink, the same as TowerTorch's own rows and buttons.
- **A focus ring you can see.** Keyboard focus is a solid ring in the accent's ink instead of a faint halo, 2px wide and 3px when your system asks for more contrast.
- **Placeholders you can read.** The hint in an empty field reaches 4.5:1 on every background.
- **Reactive buttons keep their width.** In TowerTorch's reactive mode a FireSquire button is as wide as with its glyph and words, and the words open inside it instead of the button growing under the pointer.

⚡ **Improved**
- **Fortress helpers 1.2.4.** FireSquire finds your other apps on a server with a single network interface too, and a member that already took a new phrase no longer counts as gone.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **No more false SMART failures.** The verdict reads the disk's own self-assessment, so a healthy disk that is awake no longer shows as failing.
- **The stop check names only what really holds the array.** What Unraid ends by itself is left out, and a long list of mounts shows the first five.
- **A cancelled stop is really cancelled.** Unraid's form goes back to how it was.
- **A test goes one way.** While TollRoll takes over, the test goes through TollRoll and only falls back to the channel when TollRoll does not take it.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The Health tab, the stop check and the new channel window in all 26 languages,** with seconds written the same way in every sentence of a language.
- **The Help sentence names GitHub and Discord in all 26 languages**, and French readers are addressed with tu.

## 1.4.0
✨ **Added**
- **Your fortress joins the check.** FireSquire downloads its fortress helper from ParleyPort, so the Fortress card finally shows up on the settings page. Join with your phrase, and before a reboot FireSquire asks your other apps whether one of them is still busy with something a reboot would cut short.
- **Your other apps show FireSquire with its own logo** once it has joined your fortress.
- **Take an app out of your fortress.** Every other app on the Fortress card has a Remove button. FireSquire asks first and then offers to change the phrase in an app that keeps it, because until you do, your fortress takes no new app, and one that tries to join is told why. Until then, a quiet line names who is out.
- **When another app takes FireSquire out,** the Fortress card says so, after a restart too, and once the phrase has changed, the new one brings it back in.
- **FireSquire follows a new phrase by itself.** Every 15 minutes it checks whether your fortress changed its phrase and stays the same member under the new one, even when nobody opens its page. If it cannot follow, the Fortress card asks for the new words, just like on ShipLog and TowerTorch.
- **Notifications and a schedule.** The notify helper comes along too, so you can send the verdict to Unraid, Apprise, ntfy, Gotify, Matrix, Discord, Slack, email, a webhook or Healthchecks, and run the check daily or weekly without lifting a finger.

🎨 **Design**
- **The settings page in tabs.** General, Fortress, Notifications and Info sit in a column of tiles beside the cards, just like on ShipLog's and TowerTorch's pages, and FireSquire remembers the one you had open.
- **The same Fortress card in every plugin.** FireSquire, ShipLog, TowerTorch and TollRoll share one Fortress card, in its own tab just above Info, with each app's logo and version and a status window for your other plugins.
- **FireSquire follows GlimStone 4.4.1.** While a window is open, the page behind it is out of reach for the pointer and for screen readers. A value a notification channel cannot take gets a line right under its field, and a quick answer from your fortress no longer flashes a waiting line first.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Joining and saving answer right away.** Unraid's webGUI never answers a multipart POST, so joining the fortress, leaving it and saving notifications just hung. The settings page sends its posts URL-encoded, and all three go through.
- **No more nameless rows on the fortress card.** A member that never said which app it runs, such as FireSquire's own old membership after leaving and joining again, stays off the list.
- **A tidier flash drive.** An update clears out the packages of earlier versions instead of keeping every one of them on the flash.
- **"FireSquire is ready!"** The install message had slipped into a bit of German.
- **Leaving points at the current phrase** as the way back in, since the one you left with may have changed in the meantime.
- **The name in the banner reads on a phone.** Below 600 px it runs across the watermark instead of shrinking to two letters.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The same words as the rest of your fortress, in all 26 languages.** The phrase is simply the phrase, a member is Connected or Not reachable, and Danish and Portuguese call a backup what the glossary calls it.

## 1.3.0
✨ **Added**
- **A check before every reboot.** Reboot or shut down from the WebGUI, on Main, on the Dashboard or from the update banner, and FireSquire runs its check first. All clear goes straight through. With Smouldering or The roof is on fire you see the findings and decide: reboot anyway, or cancel and keep the server up.
- **ZFS pools, btrfs devices and CRC counts.** A degraded, faulted or unavailable pool, or a device with read, write or checksum errors, sets the roof on fire. A UDMA CRC count that rose since the last check means Smouldering, and usually a bad cable or port.
- **Every disk finding names its port**, such as `sdc [pci-0000:00:17.0-ata-3]`, so you know which cable to look at. When a disk check finds something, the report links to Scrutiny if it runs on the server, or suggests it for the SMART history.
- **More of what stands in a reboot's way:** a shutdown time-out too short for the containers and VMs together, containers that ask for more stop time than Docker gives them, an unclean last shutdown, files open or being written over SMB and NFS, a UPS on battery or almost empty, and a flash backup from Unraid Connect older than a week.

🎨 **Design**
- **New names for the three levels: All clear, Smouldering and The roof is on fire.** In German they read Alles klar, Es glimmt and Feuer am Dach, and other languages use a saying of their own where they have one. The JSON keeps GO, CAUTION and NO-GO as its codes.
- **A warning beacon leads the verdict.** Its dome glows in the level's colour, and while something smoulders or burns, its beam sweeps round it, faster when the roof is on fire. With motion switched off it stays lit and still.
- **Every finding is a card**, grouped by level, with or without TowerTorch. The button to the page that fixes it shows when you point at the card.
- **The report closes from its foot** with GlimStone's close button. Every button there wears its glyph in Unraid's look too, and with no head bar and no logo, the beacon and the level have the window to themselves.
- **Buttons have rounded corners** instead of pill shapes, in both looks.
- **FireSquire follows GlimStone 4.3.7.** A button you point at or reach with the keyboard takes a touch of the accent in its ground and all of it in its glyph, and under the rainbow every button gets a colour of its own. Buttons in a window's foot keep their words instead of trailing off, and a running progress bar carries a sheen.

⚡ **Improved**
- **Ready for TowerTorch 1.4.1.** FireSquire follows TowerTorch's new internal names, so please update both together to keep the shared look.
- **A quieter flash check.** The flash write test gets a card of its own only when FireSquire cannot delete its test file.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **The texts say what the check writes.** FireSquire said it never changes anything, yet the flash check writes an empty test file to /boot and deletes it right away, and the CRC counts live in `crc.state` on the flash. The report, the settings page and the README say so now, in all 26 languages.
- **Release notes only where there is a release.** The Version card's Release notes button opens the version's own release when there is one, and the list of releases otherwise.

🌐 **Translations**
- **Everything new speaks all 26 languages**, the level names included.

## 1.2.0
✨ **Added**
- **A new name: FireSquire**, a squire who tends the beacon before your reboot, to fit the rest of the house. The check and its verdict stay as they were.
- **Check again.** With TowerTorch installed, the report window has a Check again button at its foot. It runs the check once more without closing the window.
- **The flame on the Dashboard.** A FireSquire icon sits next to Reboot in the server tile, so you can run the check right where you reboot, and it leaves no empty tile behind.
- **Version, Help and About cards** on the settings page, as on TowerTorch's and ShipLog's Info tab. The Version card shows the version without a "v", along with the GlimStone version and a Release notes button.
- **The give buttons open in the page.** Coffee, PayPal and crypto open their windows right there instead of in a new tab, and each coin's logo shows in its own colour.

🎨 **Design**
- **New logo.** The flames sit in a grey diamond that reads on light and dark themes alike, on the Plugins tab, in the menu and in the report window.
- **TowerTorch's look.** With TowerTorch installed, FireSquire takes on its look: the report window, the FireSquire button next to Reboot on the Main tab, the icon in the Dashboard's server tile and Settings → User Utilities → FireSquire. Theme, motion, labels, colours and corners follow TowerTorch's settings, and every button shows its icon.
- **The verdict in its colour.** FireSquire's logo stands right before GO, CAUTION or NO-GO, and its frame takes on the verdict's colour. Findings are grouped by tier, and each problem gets a button that opens the page where you can fix it. The note that FireSquire only advises sits behind the (i) next to the title.
- **A banner on the settings page.** Settings → User Utilities → FireSquire opens with FireSquire's logo, its name and the claim across the page. Below it, a card lists what the check looks at, with the details behind each (i).
- **Unraid's theme without TowerTorch.** The report window follows Unraid's light or dark theme instead of fixed colours.
- **A knight banner** opens the text report on the command line.

⚡ **Improved**
- **The focus stays in the report.** The keyboard focus stays inside the report window while it is open. Escape closes it, and the focus goes back to the button you opened it from.

🌐 **Translations**
- **Smoother in all 26 languages.** The report and the settings page read more smoothly, and every new label is translated.

## 1.1.0
✨ **Added**
- **A new logo, the flame in a diamond.** On the Plugins tab, in the menu and in the report window it sits in a double ring that reads on every Unraid theme, and existing installs pick it up right away. The README banner, the store icon and the support thread follow it in light and dark.
- **A live progress bar** while the check runs, with the current stage and a percentage instead of a still "Running checks" line. It follows the engine check by check, and disk by disk through the slower SMART scan. Where streaming is not available it falls back to a moving bar, so the report always appears.
- **Findings link to where you fix them.** Syslog and disk findings open the System Log, array, parity, mover, space, flash and SMART findings open Main, running VMs open the VMs tab, mount and image findings open Docker, and a stopped dockerd or libvirt opens its settings page. Only warnings and errors get a link.
- **The house banner** heads the text report when you run the engine from the console, greets you after an install and says goodbye on removal.

🎨 **Design**
- **Unraid's own font in the report window**, so it matches the rest of the WebGUI. Its progress bar takes your accent colour when TowerTorch is installed and stays amber otherwise. The verdict colours green, amber and red stay as they are.
- **The author shows as Junker der Provinz** on the Plugins tab.

⚡ **Improved**
- **The same package locally and on CI.** `plugin/pkg_build.sh` builds it with root ownership and LF line endings, and the release workflow publishes it from a version tag.

🐛 **Fixed**
- **Uninstalling removes everything.** The removal step was a bare REMOVE tag that Unraid never runs, so the files, the package and the cached copy on the flash stayed behind. A proper remove script cleans up all of it, and since the plugin runs no daemon and keeps no user data, nothing else is left.

🌐 **Translations**
- **The report follows Unraid's language** in all 26 languages it offers, where it had only English and German before, the same way ShipLog does. Arabic and Hebrew read right to left, and a string not yet translated falls back to English.

## 1.0.0
✨ **Added**
- **The pre-reboot check.** One verdict, GO, CAUTION or NO-GO, with the exact findings behind it. It is advisory only: it reads the host and reports.
- **What it looks at:** the array with its disk assignments and health, missing or degraded parity (CAUTION or NO-GO), disk and I/O errors in the syslog, and SMART early warnings.
- **A button next to Reboot** on the Main tab runs the check.

🎨 **Design**
- **In your language and theme.** The report follows Unraid's language, English or German with English as the fallback, and its light or dark theme.
- **The flame in a hazard diamond** is the logo on the Plugins tab, in the menu and in the report window, and the banner is set in Bree Serif and Lato.
  </CHANGES>

  <!-- Download the package from the GitHub release -->
  <FILE Name="&plgPath;/&pkg;-&version;.txz">
    <URL>&pkgURL;</URL>
    <SHA256>&sha256;</SHA256>
  </FILE>

  <!-- Install -->
  <FILE Run="/bin/bash">
    <INLINE>
      echo "Installing FireSquire &version; ..."
      # installpkg overwrites same-path files but does not delete files that were
      # removed between versions (the .txz name carries the version, so each is a
      # "new" package). Wipe any previous FireSquire package + the plugin dir
      # first, so stale files (e.g. an old .page) never linger.
      for p in /var/log/packages/&pkg;*; do
        if [ -e "$p" ]; then removepkg "$(basename "$p")" 2>/dev/null; fi
      done
      rm -rf "&emhttp;"
      installpkg "&plgPath;/&pkg;-&version;.txz"
      # Unraid keeps every downloaded package on the flash, and only this one is needed.
      find "&plgPath;" -maxdepth 1 -name '&pkg;-*.txz' ! -name '&pkg;-&version;.txz' -delete 2>/dev/null
      # Enforce the exec bit on the engine (NTFS/CI builds can drop it)
      chmod +x "&emhttp;/&pkg;-check.sh" 2>/dev/null
      # ParleyPort's helpers for the fortress and the notifications, kept on
      # the flash for offline boots. Without one, its part stays out, so a
      # failed download never stops the install.
      helper() {
        file="&plgPath;/$1-&helperVer;"
        find "&plgPath;" -maxdepth 1 -name "$1-v*" ! -name "$1-&helperVer;" -delete 2>/dev/null
        if [ ! -s "$file" ]; then
          if curl -fsSL --max-time 60 -o "$file.part" "&helperURL;/$1-linux-amd64" 2>/dev/null; then mv "$file.part" "$file"; fi
        fi
        if echo "$2  $file" | sha256sum -c --status 2>/dev/null; then
          install -m 0755 "$file" "&emhttp;/$1"
        else
          rm -f "$file" "$file.part"
          echo "The $1 helper could not be loaded, so FireSquire goes without it for now."
        fi
      }
      helper fortress "&fortressSHA;"
      helper notify "&notifySHA;"
      # A membership from before TollRoll's hub learns to hand it the messages.
      php -r "require '&emhttp;/include/fs_fortress.php'; fs_fortress_uses_ensure('&plgPath;/fortress');" 2>/dev/null
      # The fortress is asked for a new phrase every 15 minutes, clear of the
      # check's half past, and a schedule set on the settings page lives on
      # the flash next to it.
      cron="7,22,37,52 * * * * /bin/bash &emhttp;/&pkg;-follow.sh"
      if [ "$(cat "&plgPath;/fortress.cron" 2>/dev/null)" != "$cron" ]; then echo "$cron" > "&plgPath;/fortress.cron"; fi
      # TollRoll counts a plugin whose status is older than an hour as gone, so
      # the status of the last check is written again every hour.
      cron="47 * * * * /usr/bin/php &emhttp;/&pkg;-history.php --refresh >/dev/null 2>/dev/null"
      if [ "$(cat "&plgPath;/status.cron" 2>/dev/null)" != "$cron" ]; then echo "$cron" > "&plgPath;/status.cron"; fi
      /usr/local/sbin/update_cron 2>/dev/null
      echo ""
      cat "&emhttp;/banner.txt" 2>/dev/null
      echo ""
      echo "FireSquire is ready!"
    </INLINE>
  </FILE>

  <!-- Remove. This has to be a <FILE ... Method="remove"> block: Unraid's plugin
       manager only runs <FILE> elements, so a bare <REMOVE> tag is silently ignored
       and the cleanup never runs. It has to end with exit 0, or Unraid leaves the
       .plg on the flash and it reinstalls on boot. No daemon runs; removing the
       folder on the flash drops the channels and the schedules, and update_cron
       takes them out of cron. -->
  <FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
    <INLINE>
      # goodbye banner first, while the files still exist
      cat "&emhttp;/banner.txt" 2>/dev/null
      echo ""
      echo "Bye!"
      echo ""
      pkg=$(ls /var/log/packages/ 2>/dev/null | grep -E "^&pkg;(-[^-]+-[^-]+-[^-]+)?$" | head -n1)
      if [ -n "$pkg" ]; then removepkg "$pkg" 2>/dev/null; fi
      removepkg "&pkg;" 2>/dev/null
      rm -rf "&emhttp;"
      rm -rf "&plgPath;" /var/local/firesquire
      rm -f /var/local/fortress/firesquire.json /var/local/fortress/firesquire.*.json
      /usr/local/sbin/update_cron 2>/dev/null
      exit 0
    </INLINE>
  </FILE>

</PLUGIN>
